overlay network 
Docker Overlay Network

Overlay networking is used when you want containers running on different Docker hosts to communicate with each other as if they were on the same network.

This is especially important when teaching Docker Swarm.

First, compare bridge vs overlay
BRIDGE NETWORK
────────────────────────────────

Docker Host 1
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│                              │
│  ┌───────┐     ┌───────┐    │
│  │ App 1 │─────│ DB 1  │    │
│  └───────┘     └───────┘    │
│       Docker Bridge          │
└──────────────────────────────┘


OVERLAY NETWORK
────────────────────────────────

       Docker Host 1                  Docker Host 2
┌──────────────────────┐       ┌──────────────────────┐
│                      │       │                      │
│   ┌───────┐          │       │          ┌───────┐   │
│   │ App 1 │          │       │          │ App 2 │   │
│   └───┬───┘          │       │          └───┬───┘   │
│       │              │       │              │       │
└───────┼──────────────┘       └──────────────┼───────┘
        │                                     │
        └──────────── OVERLAY ────────────────┘

The key question to ask students:

"How can Container A on Server 1 communicate with Container B on Server 2?"

Answer:

Overlay network.

1. Why do we need Overlay Networks?

Suppose you have two EC2 instances:

EC2-1
Private IP: 10.0.1.10

EC2-2
Private IP: 10.0.2.10

And Docker containers:

EC2-1                    EC2-2

┌─────────────┐          ┌─────────────┐
│ Container A │          │ Container B │
│ 172.18.0.2  │          │ 172.18.0.2  │
└─────────────┘          └─────────────┘

A normal Docker bridge network exists only on one Docker host.

So:

Container A
     |
  Bridge
     |
   EC2-1
     
      X X X

   EC2-2
     |
  Bridge
     |
Container B

The bridge network doesn't automatically span both hosts.

An overlay network creates a logical network across the Docker hosts:

Container A
     |
     v
Docker Host 1
     |
     |  Overlay Network
     |
Docker Host 2
     |
     v
Container B
2. Overlay Network is Mainly a Swarm Concept

For your class, connect overlay networking with:

Docker Swarm
     |
     ├── Manager nodes
     ├── Worker nodes
     └── Overlay networks

A typical architecture:

                Docker Swarm
                     |
        ┌────────────┴────────────┐
        |                         |
    Manager                    Worker
    EC2-1                      EC2-2
        |                         |
   ┌────┴────┐               ┌────┴────┐
   │  App    │               │  App    │
   │Container│               │Container│
   └────┬────┘               └────┬────┘
        |                         |
        └───────────┬─────────────┘
                    |
             Overlay Network
3. Hands-on Demo

For students, I recommend doing this with two EC2 instances.

You need:

EC2-1
Docker installed

EC2-2
Docker installed

Then create a Docker Swarm.

Step 1 — Initialize Swarm

On EC2-1:

docker swarm init --advertise-addr <EC2-1-PRIVATE-IP>

You'll get something like:

docker swarm join --token SWMTKN-xxxxx \
10.0.1.10:2377
4. Join the Second Machine

On EC2-2, execute the command generated by the manager:

docker swarm join --token SWMTKN-xxxxx \
10.0.1.10:2377

Now check from EC2-1:

docker node ls

You should see something similar to:

ID        HOSTNAME    STATUS    AVAILABILITY    MANAGER STATUS
xxxx      manager     Ready     Active          Leader
xxxx      worker      Ready     Active

Now you have a Swarm cluster.

5. Create the Overlay Network

On the manager:

docker network create \
  --driver overlay \
  my-overlay

Check:

docker network ls

You'll see:

NETWORK ID     NAME          DRIVER    SCOPE
xxxx           ingress       overlay   swarm
xxxx           my-overlay    overlay   swarm

Notice:

DRIVER = overlay
SCOPE  = swarm

This is an important observation for students.

6. Create a Service

Now create a service:

docker service create \
  --name web \
  --network my-overlay \
  nginx

Check:

docker service ls

Example:

ID        NAME    MODE        REPLICAS
xxxx      web     replicated  1/1
7. Increase the Replicas

Now:

docker service scale web=3

Check:

docker service ls

You may get:

NAME    REPLICAS
web     3/3

The three containers could be distributed across your Swarm nodes:

                 my-overlay
                     |
        ┌────────────┴────────────┐
        |                         |
     EC2-1                      EC2-2
        |                         |
   ┌────┴────┐              ┌─────┴─────┐
   │ web.1   │              │ web.2     │
   │         │              │ web.3     │
   └─────────┘              └───────────┘

The overlay network allows the services to communicate across hosts.

8. The Most Important Demonstration

This is where you can make the concept very clear.

Create:

docker service create \
  --name redis \
  --network my-overlay \
  redis

Now create an application service:

docker service create \
  --name app \
  --network my-overlay \
  nginx

Architecture:

               my-overlay
                   |
       ┌───────────┴───────────┐
       |                       |
     app                     redis
       |                       |
       └───────────┬───────────┘
                   |
             Cross-host
             communication

The application can communicate with:

redis:6379

without needing Redis to be exposed publicly.

That's a very important production concept.

9. Overlay vs Bridge

Put this table on your classroom screen:

Feature	Bridge	Overlay
Scope	Single Docker host	Multiple Docker hosts
Common use	Standalone Docker	Swarm
Driver	bridge	overlay
Cross-host communication	❌	✅
Service discovery	✅ user-defined bridge	✅
Swarm support	Limited	Designed for Swarm
Typical architecture	Single server	Cluster
10. Overlay Network + Application Architecture

A great final diagram for students:

                       Users
                         |
                         v
                  Load Balancer
                         |
                         v
              ┌────────────────────┐
              │    Docker Swarm    │
              │                    │
              │  Overlay Network   │
              │                    │
              │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │
              │ │ App  │ │ App  │ │
              │ └───┬──┘ └──┬───┘ │
              │     │       │      │
              │     └───┬───┘      │
              │         │          │
              │       Redis        │
              │         │          │
              │      Database      │
              └────────────────────┘

The key idea:

The containers don't need to know which physical Docker host the other container is running on. The overlay network provides the logical connectivity.

11. Very Important: Don't Confuse -p and Overlay

Suppose you create:

docker service create \
  --name web \
  --network my-overlay \
  -p 8080:80 \
  nginx

There are two separate concepts:

--network my-overlay
        ↓
Container-to-container
communication


-p 8080:80
        ↓
External traffic
        ↓
Host/Swarm service

So tell students:

Overlay does not mean "publish the container to the Internet."

Overlay is primarily about network connectivity between workloads across Docker hosts.

12. One Excellent Classroom Experiment

Ask students this question before showing the answer:

"I have two EC2 instances. I run an nginx container on EC2-1 and a Redis container on EC2-2. I want the nginx/application container to communicate with Redis using redis:6379. What do I need?"

Let them answer.

Then reveal:

Docker Swarm
     +
Overlay Network
     +
Service Discovery

Architecture:

EC2-1                         EC2-2
─────                         ─────

App                           Redis
 │                              │
 │                              │
 └──────── my-overlay ──────────┘
                │
                ↓
          redis:6379

That gives students the why, rather than just teaching:

docker network create --driver overlay
One sentence students should remember

Bridge connects containers on the same Docker host; Overlay connects containers/services across multiple Docker hosts, typically in a Docker Swarm cluster.

And the progression for your Docker networking class can be:

Default Bridge
      ↓
Custom Bridge
      ↓
Container DNS
      ↓
Port Mapping
      ↓
Network Isolation
      ↓
Multiple Networks
      ↓
Host / None
      ↓
        ⭐ Overlay
      ↓
Docker Swarm
      ↓
Multi-host Application

For a live class, the two-EC2 Swarm + app + redis overlay demo is the one I'd use—it makes the purpose of overlay networking immediately obvious.